<h1>Bump Turn Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A bump turn conveyor uses angled rollers, skate wheels, or guide rails at a conveyor junction to redirect products around a curve without a powered transfer mechanism. The product's forward momentum carries it into the angled surface, which deflects it onto the new travel path. Bump turns handle direction changes of 30, 45, or 90 degrees and are the simplest, lowest-cost method for changing conveyor direction in <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging and distribution systems</a>.</p>
<h2 id="passive-heading">Passive Bump Turns</h2>
<p>Passive bump turns contain no motors at the turn point. The product arrives on a powered infeed conveyor at sufficient speed, contacts the angled deflection surface, and redirects onto the discharge conveyor. The only energy source is the product's forward momentum from the infeed conveyor.</p>
<h3>Angled Skate Wheel Bump Turn</h3>
<p>The most common passive bump turn uses skate wheels mounted on axles angled toward the discharge direction. As the product contacts the angled wheels, the wheels spin and guide the product through the turn. Skate wheel bump turns handle light to moderate loads (1-75 lbs) and work best with flat-bottomed products that span multiple wheel rows. Hytrol and Intelligrated offer skate wheel curve sections that integrate with their <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyor</a> platforms.</p>
<p>Skate wheel bump turns require a minimum infeed speed of 60-80 FPM for reliable product tracking through the curve. Slower speeds risk products stalling in the turn. Heavier products need higher infeed speeds to maintain momentum through friction losses at the skate wheels.</p>
<h3>Dead Roller Bump Turn</h3>
<p>Dead (non-powered) rollers mounted at an angle provide a bump turn surface for heavier products. The product slides across the angled rollers, which spin freely to reduce friction. Dead roller bump turns handle 10-200 lbs and suit products with rigid, flat bottoms. The roller diameter and spacing must support the product's bottom surface without the product dropping between rollers.</p>
<h3>Guide Rail Deflection</h3>
<p>The simplest bump turn is a curved guide rail positioned at the outside of a <a href="/conveyor-types/curved-conveyors/">conveyor curve</a>. The product's leading edge contacts the rail, and the rail redirects the product along the curve. Guide rail deflection works on <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller conveyors</a> and belt conveyors where the conveying surface provides the driving force and the rail provides directional guidance. Guide rail curves cost the least but generate the most product impact force and are unsuitable for fragile goods.</p>
<h2 id="powered-heading">Powered Bump Turns</h2>
<p>Powered bump turns add motorized rollers or driven wheels to the turn section. The powered elements actively drive the product through the curve, eliminating dependence on infeed momentum. Powered turns handle heavier products, lower infeed speeds, and provide consistent transit time through the curve.</p>
<h3>Tapered Roller Curve</h3>
<p>Tapered roller curves use conical rollers that are wider at the outside radius and narrower at the inside. The taper differential drives the product around the curve. A single motor and chain or belt drives all rollers. Tapered roller curves handle 10-500 lbs and maintain consistent product speed from infeed to discharge. Hytrol, Dematic, and mk North America manufacture tapered roller curve sections in 30, 45, 60, and 90-degree configurations.</p>
<p>Tapered rollers provide the best product control through curves for case-sized products on <a href="/conveyor-types/live-roller-conveyors/">live roller conveyor</a> systems. The taper prevents the product from drifting to the outside of the curve, a common problem with straight-roller curves driven by friction contact.</p>
<h3>Belt-Over-Roller Curve</h3>
<p>Belt-over-roller curves use a flat belt riding over a curved roller bed. The belt provides a continuous driven surface that carries the product through the turn. Belt curves handle irregular product shapes, small items, and polybags that cannot ride on rollers. Dorner and mk North America offer precision belt curve modules for small-product packaging applications.</p>
<h3>Powered Wheel Curve</h3>
<p>Powered wheel curves use individually driven wheels mounted at angles within the curve. Each wheel has a small motor or is driven by a common drive through differential gearing. Powered wheel curves provide omnidirectional drive forces that guide the product through complex curve profiles. They suit distribution center applications where <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR conveyor</a> systems need powered curves between straight sections.</p>
<h2 id="orientation-heading">Case Orientation Effects</h2>
<p>Bump turns rotate product orientation. A 90-degree bump turn rotates the product 90 degrees relative to the conveyor center line. The leading edge becomes a side edge. A 12x18-inch case traveling long-side-first (18" leading) through a 90-degree bump turn exits with the short side leading (12" leading). This rotation affects downstream operations.</p>
<h3>Intentional Orientation Change</h3>
<p>Some applications intentionally use bump turns for case rotation. Rotating a case 90 degrees before a label applicator exposes a different face for labeling. Rotating before a <a href="/conveyor-functions/collating-laning/">collating station</a> changes the packing orientation for case loading. Rotating before palletizing changes the layer pattern. Bump turns provide a zero-cost orientation change compared to dedicated case turners.</p>
<h3>Undesirable Orientation Change</h3>
<p>Barcode readers, vision systems, and print-and-apply labelers require consistent product orientation. A bump turn before a barcode scanner moves the barcode from the leading face to the side, requiring scanner repositioning or additional scanners. If orientation must stay constant through a direction change, use a <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">pop-up chain transfer or turntable</a> that moves the product sideways without rotation.</p>
<h3>Predictable vs. Unpredictable Rotation</h3>
<p>Rectangular products with flat bottoms rotate predictably through bump turns: the rotation angle equals the turn angle. Round products (bottles, cans, drums) may spin unpredictably on bump turn surfaces, especially on skate wheels. Irregularly shaped products may tip, jam, or rotate inconsistently. Test product behavior on bump turn samples before committing to a layout design. Powered belt curves provide the most predictable rotation for non-rectangular products.</p>
