<h2 id="apps-heading">Bump Turn Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Packaging line direction changes:</strong> Bump turns redirect conveyor flow around building columns, walls, and equipment obstructions. A 90-degree powered tapered roller curve fits into an L-shaped <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line layout</a> and maintains line speed through the turn. Multiple 30-degree turns create gentle S-curves that route conveyors around obstacles with less orientation change per turn.</p>
<p><strong>Gravity conveyor systems:</strong> Passive skate wheel and dead roller bump turns redirect products on <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/gravity/">gravity roller conveyor</a> systems. The gravity decline provides the momentum to carry products through the turn. Shipping and receiving areas commonly use gravity conveyors with bump turns to route packages from truck doors to staging areas without power requirements.</p>
<p><strong>Case rotation for labeling:</strong> A 90-degree bump turn before a label applicator rotates the case to expose the target labeling face. This eliminates the need for a dedicated case turner. Two sequential 90-degree bump turns rotate a case 180 degrees (end-for-end). Layout designers use bump turns as no-cost case orientation tools.</p>
<p><strong>Distribution center conveyor networks:</strong> Large distribution centers route conveyors through multi-level pick modules with numerous direction changes. Powered tapered roller curves and belt curves handle the heavy, continuous traffic at 100-200 FPM. <a href="/conveyor-types/mdr-conveyors/">MDR systems</a> from TGW, Dematic, and Bastian Solutions include powered curve modules that drop in alongside straight MDR sections without bridging hardware.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/conveyor-types/flexible-conveyors/">Flexible conveyor</a> alternatives:</strong> Expandable skate wheel conveyors inherently provide bump turn capability at any angle along their length. Workers position the flexible conveyor in curves to route products from truck unloading to staging areas. The skate wheels serve as built-in bump turn surfaces at every curve point.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">How to Select a Bump Turn</h2>
<p><strong>Passive vs. powered is the first decision.</strong> Use passive bump turns when infeed speed exceeds 60 FPM, products weigh under 75 lbs, and products have flat bottoms. Use powered bump turns when infeed speed is variable or slow, products weigh over 75 lbs, or products require consistent speed through the curve.</p>
<p><strong>Product bottom surface determines the mechanism.</strong> Flat, rigid bottoms work on skate wheels and rollers. Flexible bottoms (polybags, pouches) require belt curves. Small products that fall between rollers require belt curves or closely spaced skate wheels. Products with protruding features on the bottom may catch on roller or wheel gaps.</p>
<p><strong>Consider the orientation change.</strong> Every bump turn rotates the product by the turn angle. If this rotation is unacceptable, use a <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/pivot/">pivot conveyor</a> or pop-up transfer instead. If the rotation is acceptable or desirable, bump turns provide the lowest-cost direction change. Plan the barcode position and label orientation around the bump turn rotation.</p>
<p><strong>Radius affects footprint and product behavior.</strong> Tighter radii (inside radius 12-24 inches) handle small products but generate more product rotation stress. Wider radii (inside radius 36-60 inches) provide smoother product flow but consume more floor space. Standard tapered roller curves from Hytrol and Dematic come in 12, 18, 24, 36, and 48-inch inside radii.</p>
<p><strong><a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation zones</a> should not include bump turns.</strong> Products accumulating in a curve experience uneven backpressure and may jam, tip, or rotate unpredictably. Place accumulation sections on straight conveyor runs before or after the bump turn, not within the curve.</p>
<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a bump turn conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A bump turn uses angled rollers, skate wheels, or guide rails to redirect products around a curve. The product's forward momentum carries it into the angled surface, deflecting it onto the new path. See our <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">transfer conveyor guide</a> for all direction-change methods.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the difference between passive and powered bump turns?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Passive bump turns have no motors and rely on product momentum. Powered bump turns use motorized tapered rollers or belts to drive products through the curve. Passive turns cost less but need higher infeed speeds and lighter products. Powered turns handle heavier loads at any speed.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">Does a bump turn change product orientation?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Yes. The product rotates by the turn angle. A 90-degree bump turn rotates the product 90 degrees. If orientation must stay constant, use a <a href="/conveyor-types/transfer-conveyors/">pop-up transfer or turntable</a> that moves the product laterally without rotation.</p>

</div>
